SOAPBOX: Another contest and an exciting weekend are behind me. What can I say? What makes these competitions so
SOAPBOX: fascinating is seeing what can be achieved with modest equipment: a beam at just 10 meters, verticals
SOAPBOX: on 80 and 40 meters, and low power using a FlexRadio 6600 and Maestro.
SOAPBOX: After the excellent CONDX last year and a #2 finish in the Classic Low Power category with a
SOAPBOX:Zone 14 record, I felt it was time to try something new: SO2R in the low-power category.
SOAPBOX: For this event, I put up a Hexbeam at 10 meters, which I used to cover the second direction.
SOAPBOX:It actually performed quite well, especially considering it's not exactly a top-tier contest antenna, HI.
SOAPBOX:On 40 meters, I also used a dedicated dipole for Europe.
SOAPBOX: The forecast before the contest wasn’t particularly encouraging. Band conditions were poor,
SOAPBOX:especially on 10 meters, so throughout the contest I kept checking 10 meters at regular intervals,
SOAPBOX:hoping to catch the right opening and squeeze in a few extra QSOs and multipliers when the band came alive.
SOAPBOX: Saturday started in the usual fashion on 40 and 80 meters, as 20 meters was already closed.
SOAPBOX:After sunrise, I worked VK/ZL as well as FK8 on 20 meters. The rate was fantastic,
SOAPBOX:reaching a peak of 40 QSOs per hour :D, although that's pretty much what you would expect when
SOAPBOX:running low power.
SOAPBOX: Fifteen meters wasn't particularly impressive, while 10 meters opened only briefly,
SOAPBOX:allowing me to work a few stations from the south, including two V5s and 5Z4VJ (Jambo, nice QSO! :))) ).
SOAPBOX: Conditions improved somewhat during the afternoon. Twenty meters remained open to the USA
SOAPBOX:until quite late, but most of my contacts were made in S&P mode rather than by running.
SOAPBOX: I decided to take a four-hour break during the night and resumed operating around 0330Z.
SOAPBOX: Sunday was a strange day. At times, the rate dropped to just 15 to 20 QSOs per hour,
SOAPBOX:and more than once I found myself wondering why I was putting myself through all of this :D.
SOAPBOX: Ten meters opened briefly, and 9J2FI was QRV with an excellent signal,
SOAPBOX:but the pileup was simply too large to break through. A few Caribbean stations were audible
SOAPBOX:for a short period, and I managed to log KP4 and P4. I also heard a W station working P49X,
SOAPBOX:listening on the skew path at around 250°, but unfortunately I wasn't able to work a
SOAPBOX:single USA station myself.
SOAPBOX: On Sunday afternoon, I picked up a few juicy multipliers from the Far East on 20 meters,
SOAPBOX:including RU0LL (Zone 19), E2M, and 7C1C.
SOAPBOX:However, my personal highlight of the contest was undoubtedly ZL3IO, who managed to hear me
SOAPBOX:and complete a QSO on 80 meters.
SOAPBOX: Although his signal was only peaking intermittently on my vertical, we were able to make it
SOAPBOX:through and complete the contact. That QSO alone made my day.
SOAPBOX: TNX, Holger!
SOAPBOX: In total, I was QRV for about 38 hours. I ended up taking five breaks of roughly 30 minutes each,
SOAPBOX:simply because the rate just wasn't there. I also decided to go QRT at 2100Z,
SOAPBOX:as Monday is a working day and I still need to finish preparing equipment for our upcoming H49A DXpedition.
SOAPBOX: Despite the challenging conditions, the SO2R low-power experiment was a lot of fun
SOAPBOX:and provided a valuable learning experience. There is definitely room for improvement,
SOAPBOX:but that's exactly what keeps contesting interesting.
SOAPBOX:Now it's time to shift the focus to H49A and look forward to the next adventure.
SOAPBOX: Equipment: Flexradio 6600, Maestro console,4O3A Antenna Genius, 4O3A HP BPF
SOAPBOX: Antennas:
SOAPBOX: 80m UA2FZ Vertical
SOAPBOX: 40m Vertical, dipole
SOAPBOX: 20-15-10m: LZA 10-5 Beam @10m high (2 el. per band), Hexbeam @10m high
